::ENG3_U04_L03_Q01::[MLA.ENG3.RS.01] Research question: How does the image of an empty town square develop community memory? Passage: The fountain is dry, but the stone rim is smooth from many hands. Source: Public monuments in literature often preserve shared history through physical traces. Which synthesis is strongest? {
~A. The fountain is dry, and public monuments are in literature.#This only places facts side by side. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~B. The stone rim has been touched by many hands, so the town should repair the fountain.#This shifts to a practical recommendation. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=C. The smooth stone rim supports the idea of community memory because the source explains that physical traces on monuments can preserve shared history.#Correct. This combines source information to support the interpretation. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~D. The source proves that all fountains are monuments.#This overgeneralizes the source. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}
::ENG3_U04_L03_Q02::[MLA.ENG3.R.10] Source: Literary settings can suggest history when objects show evidence of repeated human use. Which paraphrase is most accurate? {
=A. A setting may reveal the past when objects show signs that people used them over time.#Correct. This restates the idea accurately. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~B. Objects in literature are only important when many people use them.#This adds an unsupported limitation. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~C. Human use ruins objects in literary settings.#This changes the meaning of the source. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. Settings are more important than characters in historical literature.#This introduces a comparison the source does not make. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}
::ENG3_U04_L03_Q03::[MLA.ENG3.RS.01] Passage: A theater stage is dark, but circles on the floor show where performers once stood. Source: Performance spaces can preserve public memory by holding traces of past voices and audiences. Which sentence best synthesizes the sources? {
~A. The stage is dark, and performance spaces are important.#This is too general and does not explain the connection. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~B. The performers stood in circles on the stage floor.#This misreads the detail as performers standing in circles. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~C. The source is about voices, while the passage is about a stage.#This separates the sources instead of connecting them. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=D. The floor marks support an interpretation about public memory because the research source explains that performance spaces preserve traces of past voices and audiences.#Correct. This connects primary evidence and secondary context. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
}
::ENG3_U04_L03_Q04::[MLA.ENG3.W.02] Passage: A closed station has dusty ticket windows and benches worn smooth by travelers. Source: Transit spaces in literature can symbolize transition, loss, and connection. Which analytical sentence uses both sources well? {
